From e1783cb225cdec6afe01b1c37df2d098718a273d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 10:42:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ctr: let --platform choose the spec's platform on Windows

`ctr run --runtime io.containerd.nerdbox.v1 --snapshotter erofs <linux image>`
on a Windows host produces a **Windows** OCI spec for a Linux image, and no
flag changes that.

cmd/ctr/commands/run/run_windows.go picks the spec's platform from the
snapshotter name and nothing else:

    if snapshotter == "windows-lcow" {
        opts = append(opts, oci.WithDefaultSpecForPlatform("linux/amd64"))
        // Clear the rootfs section.
        opts = append(opts, oci.WithRootFSPath(""))
    } else {
        opts = append(opts, oci.WithDefaultSpec())
        opts = append(opts, oci.WithWindowNetworksAllowUnqualifiedDNSQuery())
        opts = append(opts, oci.WithWindowsIgnoreFlushesDuringBoot())
    }

The --platform flag `ctr run` advertises -- "Run image for specific platform"
-- is read only by run_unix.go, for the spec (lines 104-119, 141) and for image
resolution (lines 162-168). On Windows it is accepted and silently ignored for
both.

That was reasonable while the only Linux guest on a Windows host was LCOW,
whose snapshotter name doubles as a declaration of intent. It stops being
reasonable as soon as a runtime runs Linux guests from some other snapshotter.
A microVM runtime backed by the erofs snapshotter gets a spec with
`s.Windows != nil, s.Linux == nil`, so oci.WithImageConfigArgs takes its
Windows branch and the guest is handed a config.json with no `linux` section
for a Linux image.

There is no way to ask for anything else. `ctr run --config <spec.json>` is the
only other route to a chosen spec, and on this path it skips
containerd.WithNewSnapshot entirely, so the task is created with an empty
Rootfs -- client/container.go's handleMounts returns without adding any mount
when SnapshotKey is "". A correct spec and a rootfs are mutually exclusive
today.

So --platform now selects the spec's platform here as it does on Unix, and
constrains image resolution the same way run_unix.go does: client.GetImage
matches the host platform, and a linux/amd64 image pulled onto a Windows host
has no windows/amd64 manifest for it to find.

Nothing that works today changes. With no --platform, windows-lcow still
implies linux/amd64 and still clears the rootfs path -- that is an LCOW
property, not a Linux one, so it stays keyed to the snapshotter -- and every
other invocation still gets the Windows spec plus the two Windows-only spec
options, which are also still applied when --platform names a Windows platform.

## Choosing the platform is not enough: the `windows` section has to go too

Measured on Windows 11, 2026-08-14. With the above, containerd started the
shim, booted the microVM, and the guest mounted the container's rootfs -- and
then crun refused the spec:

    load `config.json`: Required field 'layerFolders' not present

A Linux spec generated on a Windows host carries a `windows` section.
generateDefaultSpecWithPlatform (pkg/oci/spec.go:97-100) ends its non-Windows
branch with

    if err == nil && runtime.GOOS == "windows" {
        // To run LCOW we have a Linux and Windows section. Add an empty one now.
        s.Windows = &specs.Windows{}
    }

which is unconditional in the host's OS and blind to the guest's runtime.
`omitempty` does not drop it: specs.Spec.Windows is a *pointer*, and a non-nil
pointer to a zero struct is not empty to encoding/json. specs.Windows.LayerFolders
carries no omitempty of its own, so what is marshalled -- and what nerdbox
forwards into the guest verbatim -- is `"windows":{"layerFolders":null}`. crun
parses that with libocispec, whose generated parser checks a schema's required
fields whenever the object holding them is present, and runtime-spec's
schema/config-windows.json makes layerFolders required. The section is optional;
its contents are not. So the "empty and harmless" object is fatal.

It is removed rather than filled in: a Linux guest has no layer folders, and a
value invented to satisfy a parser is a lie the guest then has to ignore.

Only for a non-Windows platform with a snapshotter that is not windows-lcow --
the case this patch created and nothing else can reach. LCOW keeps its section,
because the runhcs shim is what fills LayerFolders in.

The option is applied **last**, and that is load-bearing. containerd's own
image-config options read `s.Windows != nil` as "this is LCOW, the guest
resolves users and groups for itself": oci.WithAdditionalGIDs returns early
(spec_opts.go:873-876) and oci.WithUser uses Process.User.Username as a holding
spot (spec_opts.go:624-627). Both alternatives mount the image's snapshot on the
host, and mount_windows.go rejects any mount whose type is not "windows-layer".
Removing the section before oci.WithImageConfig has run would trade
`Required field 'layerFolders' not present` for `invalid windows mount type: erofs`
one step earlier.

The flags that write into the Windows section and nowhere else -- --cni,
--isolated, --cpu-count, --cpu-shares, --cpu-max, --device -- would be discarded
along with it, so they are now refused rather than ignored on a non-Windows
spec. --memory-limit is not among them: oci.WithMemoryLimit writes both sections
when both exist, and the Linux one survives.

withoutWindowsSection lives in guestspec.go, with no build constraint, purely so
that it can be tested somewhere other than Windows. guestspec_test.go builds the
spec a Windows host would have produced by hand -- the condition is the host's
own OS, which no test can choose -- and asserts both halves: that it marshals as
the text crun rejected, and that the option removes it and disturbs nothing else.
